WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.163
Some changes last milliseconds, not hours.

2
00:00:07.023 --> 00:00:08.103
Do they follow the same rule?

3
00:00:09.117 --> 00:00:13.877
No. The facilitation and depression shown here are a different machine.

4
00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:19.177
They need no postsynaptic coincidence, and they work mainly in the presynaptic

5
00:00:19.177 --> 00:00:23.457
terminal. Short-term plasticity also includes other mechanisms.

6
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:26.450
Give two pulses close together.

7
00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.110
Divide the second response by the first.

8
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.370
That is the paired-pulse ratio.

9
00:00:32.433 --> 00:00:34.193
Above one is facilitation.

10
00:00:35.153 --> 00:00:36.153
Below one is depression.

11
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:42.693
The classic explanation for facilitation is residual calcium, left in the terminal

12
00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:43.693
from the first spike.

13
00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:50.003
In 2016, Jackman, Regehr and colleagues deleted synaptotagmin 7 in mice.

14
00:00:51.043 --> 00:00:55.923
Facilitation was lost at the synapses tested, though residual calcium was unchanged.

15
00:00:57.017 --> 00:01:02.617
So at those synapses, synaptotagmin 7 is a sensor that reads the leftover calcium.

16
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.730
Depression has a simple candidate.

17
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.230
The first spike uses up vesicles.

18
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:14.770
In a simple depletion model, with no refilling and no facilitation, the first pulse

19
00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.470
releases 40% % of the pool.

20
00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.430
Will the second response be larger or smaller?

21
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:31.953
Smaller. 60% % of the pool is left, so the second response is 60% % of the first.

22
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:35.737
But a ratio like that does not prove depletion.

23
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:38.777
Other processes can give the same number.

24
00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:43.707
Now back to Hebb. Does memory actually use LTP?

25
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.690
A correlation, learning alongside potentiation, is not proof.

26
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:54.793
In 1986, Morris and colleagues infused an NMDA blocker into rats.

27
00:01:55.853 --> 00:02:00.853
Place learning was impaired, a visual task was spared, and hippocampal LTP was

28
00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:01.853
suppressed.

29
00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:04.027
But the drug reached many synapses.

30
00:02:04.987 --> 00:02:08.987
It showed the receptors were needed for both, not that the learning was LTP.

31
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:16.020
In 2014, Nabavi, Malinow and colleagues used light on auditory inputs to the rat

32
00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:17.920
lateral amygdala, as a cue.

33
00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:22.653
After light was paired with shock, the light suppressed lever pressing.

34
00:02:23.953 --> 00:02:26.573
Optical LTD of those inputs removed the response.

35
00:02:27.683 --> 00:02:30.283
Optical LTP afterwards restored it.

36
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:35.047
Is LTP at these inputs enough, on its own, to create the memory?

37
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:47.250
No. In rats never given the pairing, LTP at these inputs produced no response.

38
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.350
Something set up by the pairing had to be there.

39
00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.710
That holds for this pathway, in rats, in this task.

40
00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:59.033
The takeaway: fast changes are mostly a separate, presynaptic machine.

41
00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:05.373
And linking LTP to memory takes tests of necessity and sufficiency, not correlation

42
00:03:05.373 --> 00:03:05.753
alone.
